HW1 — Prompting & Structured Output
Covers: Lectures 1–2 — prompt engineering (chat roles, temperature/top_p sampling, zero/few-shot, chain-of-thought, streaming) and structured output (JSON mode / response schemas, Pydantic & function-calling schemas, validation & repair).

Goal
By the end of this homework you can do the two things every LLM app depends on: get the model to say what you want, and turn its reply into data your code can trust. You'll reason about sampling settings, write the two stdlib helpers that make a "prompt + validate + repair" pipeline survive a real (imperfect) model, and design a prompt that holds up under iteration.

Everything here builds toward the same pattern you'll reuse for the rest of the course: a chat call whose output is parsed into a typed object before the rest of your program touches it. Remember the course caveat — our class proxy ignores response_format (both json_object and json_schema), so you cannot lean on provider JSON mode. The robust path is to prompt precisely, then parse defensively.

