Explain the retrieve-then-read pattern in RAG and why citations matter.

In your own words (a) describe the steps that run at query time, (b) explain why the model "never sees the whole corpus," and (c) give two reasons citations are worth returning with an answer.

At query time, RAG first embeds the user’s question, then searches the document database for the most relevant chunks. These chunks are added to the prompt, and the LLM uses them to generate the final answer.

The model never sees the whole corpus because only the most relevant chunks are retrieved and sent to it. This saves context space and makes the answer more focused.

Citations are important because they show where the information came from and let users verify the answer. They also make the system more trustworthy and easier to debug when an answer is wrong.
